<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with 
<br class="ltx_break">Multi-A Shared Adaptation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qin Dong
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuntian Tang<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Heming Jia
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yunhang Shen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bohan Jia
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wenxuan Huang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lianyue Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiao Xie
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shaohui Lin
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has emerged as a dominant method in Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) for large language models, which augments the transformer layer with one down-projection <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and one up-projection <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. However, LoRA’s reliance on a single down-projection matrix (<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>) creates a representational bottleneck, as this solitary feature extractor is inherently insufficient for capturing the diverse signals required by complex tasks. This motivates our architectural shift to focus on enriching the feature adaptation to improve the downstream task adaptation ability. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MASA</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ulti-<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>hared <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptation), an architecture that implements a “multi-<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, single-<math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” structure where the multi-<math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> expert ensemble is asymmetrically shared across layers to ensure parameter efficiency. In MASA, these specialized experts capture diverse features, which are then integrated by a single, layer-specific <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix. The effectiveness and versatility of our method are validated through a comprehensive suite of experiments spanning multi-domain generalization, single-domain specialization, and multi-task reasoning. For example, on the MMLU benchmark, MASA achieves an average accuracy of 59.62%, outperforming the standard LoRA by 1.08 points (a relative improvement of 1.84%) with comparable learnable parameters of 0.52%.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>East China Normal University</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Sanming University</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>Xiamen University</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06005v1/architectural_comparison.svg" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/132;" width="698" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Architectures of LoRA variants and our proposed MASA.
(a) <span id="Sx1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA</span>: Each fine‑tuned layer is augmented with a single pair of low‑rank adapters, one down‑projection <math id="Sx1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and one up‑projection <math id="Sx1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
(b) <span id="Sx1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA+MoE</span>: Model capacity is increased by instantiating <math id="Sx1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> independent adapter pairs <math id="Sx1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i},B_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i},B_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(c) <span id="Sx1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HydraLoRA</span>: Several up‑projection heads <math id="Sx1.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> share a common down‑projection <math id="Sx1.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a “single‑<math id="Sx1.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, multi‑<math id="Sx1.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” topology for parameter reuse.
(d) <span id="Sx1.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ours</span>: We employ one shared up‑projection <math id="Sx1.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with multiple down‑projections <math id="Sx1.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which offers a balanced trade‑off between efficiency and representational capacity.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aydın and Karaarslan 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2023</a>)</cite> have demonstrated remarkable generalization capabilities across diverse tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raiaan et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, largely attributed to their ever-increasing scale.
Adapting these powerful models to specific downstream applications is commonly achieved through fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, conventional Full Fine-Tuning (FFT) approaches update all model parameters, making them computationally expensive and memory-intensive, thereby posing significant challenges for resource-limited deployments.
As a solution, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pfeiffer et al. 2020</a>)</cite> has been proposed to keep the majority of the pre-trained model’s parameters frozen and adjust only a small subset of new or existing parameters.
Among various PEFT methods such as Adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Houlsby et al. 2019</a>)</cite> and Prefix-Tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang 2021</a>)</cite>, Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> has gained particular prominence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LoRA, a simple yet effective PEFT method, hypothesizes that the changes in weight during adaptation tend to be low-rank, which is implemented by injecting trainable low-rank matrices into transformer layers.
However, LoRA (shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a)) faces a fundamental low-rank bottleneck that consistently leads to performance gaps compared to full fine-tuning, particularly on complex reasoning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Biderman et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. This limitation stems from the assumption of low-rank updating weights, which may not hold for tasks requiring substantial representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. When practitioners encounter insufficient performance, enlarging the rank number remains an alternative solution, but this directly increases the parameter and computational overhead, potentially compromising LoRA’s efficiency advantages.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate LoRA’s limitations, the research community has explored various LoRA variants.
Initially, researchers pursued capacity expansion through the Mixture-of-Experts technique (<em id="Sx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, LoRAMoE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al. 2024</a>)</cite> (shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b)), which employs multiple parallel LoRA modules to decouple multi-task information. Although this enhances representational diversity by allowing different experts to specialize in distinct tasks, the independence of these experts limits the capture of shared knowledge and incurs substantial computation and parameter overhead. Subsequently, asymmetric architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> emerged as a more parameter-efficient solution, exemplified by HydraLoRA’s “one-<math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, multi-<math id="Sx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” architecture, as shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c). This design is motivated by the observation that the down-projection matrix (<math id="Sx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>) tends to learn shared, general features by adapting to diverse data, while the up-projection matrix (<math id="Sx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>) captures task-specific variations. This paradigm of allocating representational complexity to the matrix <math id="Sx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> has become a prevailing design philosophy. However, such a “single-<math id="Sx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, multi-<math id="Sx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” strategy may introduce a potential representation bottleneck by relying on a single, low-rank <math id="Sx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix for all feature extraction. It appears that a single down-projection imposes a ceiling on the mutual information between the input and the update, thereby compressing every example into at most rank <math id="Sx1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal directions (in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Information Ceiling). ‣ Representational Bottleneck of Single-𝐴 LoRA ‣ The Proposed Method ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Empirical results corroborate this phenomenon: as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, a single‑<math id="Sx1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrix maps examples from three disparate tasks into largely overlapping regions, whereas an ensemble of experts <math id="Sx1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> produces well‑separated clusters suitable for multi-task adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The above analysis motivates us to overcome LoRA’s representational bottleneck, requiring multiple down-projection matrices (<math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-experts) to capture diverse shared features, as shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (d).
Meanwhile, the up-projection <math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is used to integrate this knowledge for multi-domain and multi-task adaptations.
To maintain architectural simplicity and computational efficiency, we employ only one up-projection matrix, which is demonstrated to be sufficient for effective adaptation while significantly reducing parameter overhead in experiments (Tab. <a href="#Sx4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ Experiments ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Motivated by the observations of feature similarity across adjacent transformer layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. 2019</a>)</cite> and further corroborated by our adjacent-layer CKA analysis (Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we find that the outputs of <math id="Sx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-experts are highly redundant across layers, indicating that these experts can be shared across layer groups.
To this end, we consider designing a parameter-efficient sharing mechanism, which not only improves adaptation efficiency but also enables the model to learn more generalized features.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Guided by these design principles, we concretize them into Multi‑<math id="Sx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Shared Adaptation (MASA)—a compact yet effective adapter which breaks the representational bottleneck in LoRA by enriching the feature adaptation rather than simply adding multiple projection heads. As depicted in Fig. <a href="#Sx2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Cross-Layer Parameter Sharing Strategies ‣ Related Work ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, MASA employs a Multi-<math id="Sx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Expert (MAE) design with a cross-layer sharing mechanism. MAE enables the model to extract diverse domain and task-specific features through an ensemble of experts <math id="Sx1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, an Asymmetric Cross-layer Sharing (ACS) is proposed to reduce expert redundancy across layers, thereby implementing efficient adaptation.
The main contributions are summarized as:</p>
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose Multi-<math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Shared Adaptation (MASA), a novel asymmetric PEFT architecture that employs a “multi-<math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, single-<math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” structure to address the representational bottleneck in conventional LoRA methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce an Asymmetric Cross-layer Sharing (ACS) strategy that enables substantial parameter efficiency by sharing the <math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix ensemble across adjacent layers while preserving layer-specific matrix <math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comprehensive experiments on multiple benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed MASA achieves superior performance with parameter efficiency, compared to existing PEFT methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06005v1/t-sne.png" id="Sx1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/521;" width="698" height="521" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
The t-SNE visualization of task-specific features extracted from the V-projection layer of the 11<sup id="Sx1.F2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="Sx1.F2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">th</span></sup> layer in the LLaMA3-8B model, comparing LoRA and three selected experts of our method after fine-tuning on OpenOrca.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx1.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06005v1/final_simi.png" id="Sx1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/597;" width="698" height="597" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Adjacent-layer similarity analysis of LoRA modules using the CKA method. (Top) Heatmaps of CKA similarity scores between adjacent layers for LoRA <math id="Sx1.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix outputs (Top left) and full LoRA increments (Top right). (Bottom left) Bar chart of average similarity scores by module type for <math id="Sx1.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix outputs versus LoRA increments. (Bottom right) Line plots of layer-wise similarity trends between consecutive layers throughout the network depth.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">LoRA Architecture and Its Variants</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) has become essential for adapting Large Language Models (LLMs) to downstream tasks. Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> only adds and updates one trainable pair of low-rank matrices <math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in each layer during fine-tuning, while keeping the existing weights frozen. It has been one of the most effective solutions in PEFT. However, the monolithic nature of a single LoRA module, with its simple one-to-one connection, limits its capacity to capture the diverse features required for complex tasks.
This limitation spurred the development of Multi-LoRA architectures presented in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
An early attempt, LoRAMoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, employs a Mixture-of-Experts framework with multiple LoRA modules. Although this enhances representational diversity, the independence of its experts impedes the learning of shared, domain-general knowledge.
Alternatively, subsequent research shifted towards asymmetric structures that share certain components.
Works like HydraLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and MTL-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> adopted a “one-<math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, multi-<math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” paradigm, where a shared matrix <math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> captures common features and multiple matrices <math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> learn task-specific projections.
Even more flexible structures like CoLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, Yao, and Chen 2025</a>)</cite>, which allow for many-to-many relationships, still predominantly adhere to a “few-<math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, many-<math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” philosophy.
This places the primary burden of feature extraction on a smaller number of <math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices, which can limit the model’s ability to capture the diverse and nuanced signals required for complex tasks. Our work explores an alternative approach by shifting the architecture to focus on the feature extraction stage. We employ a “multi-<math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, single-<math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” structure, utilizing an ensemble of <math id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices to enrich the feature representations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Cross-Layer Parameter Sharing Strategies</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter sharing across layers has emerged as a fundamental strategy for reducing memory footprint and computational overhead while maintaining performance. ALBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan et al. 2019</a>)</cite> achieved up to 90% parameter reduction in BERT by reusing attention and feed-forward weights across layers, while the Universal Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al. 2018</a>)</cite> formalized depth as recurrent time steps with shared parameters. Similar successes in computer vision (LaViT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>) and inference optimization (KVSharer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>) demonstrate reductions of up to 4× in parameters and 30% in memory usage, respectively.
Building upon these insights, the LoRA community has explored parameter sharing strategies tailored to low-rank adaptation. As models scale, the cumulative parameter count across layers has motivated sharing investigations. Recent approaches include VeRA’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kopiczko, Blankevoort, and Asano 2023</a>)</cite> sharing of random matrices, VB-LoRA’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, Han, and Ji 2024</a>)</cite> vector-bank reconstruction, and BSLoRA’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. 2025</a>)</cite> granular slicing schemes.
However, these methods face a fundamental limitation: their strategies assume symmetric LoRA structures, treating the <math id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices equivalently. This prevents them from exploiting the functional asymmetry between feature extraction and projection identified.
Guided by empirical analysis of inter-layer LoRA similarity, our method shares the down-projection matrices (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>) across layer groups while preserving layer-specific up-projection matrices (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06005v1/final.drawio.svg" id="Sx2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/341;" width="698" height="341"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
An overview of our proposed MASA architecture. Each transformer layer is augmented with a shared set of <math id="Sx2.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> modules and layer-specific <math id="Sx2.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> modules. The shared <math id="Sx2.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> modules are managed by a Multi-<math id="Sx2.F4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Expert (MAE) block, enabling inter-layer sharing for improved parameter efficiency via an Asymmetric Cross-layer Sharing (ACS). Pretrained weights (<math id="Sx2.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>) are frozen while only <math id="Sx2.F4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}^{(k)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}^{(k)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Sx2.F4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\lfloor l/S\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\lfloor l/S\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="Sx2.F4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated during fine-tuning, where <math id="Sx2.F4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the group size.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">The Proposed Method</h2>

<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> posits that the additional and updated weight <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> is low-rank for a pre-trained weight matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> during fine-tuning. Specifically,
<math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposed into the low-rank matrices, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the rank <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(d_{in},d_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(d_{in},d_{out})</annotation></semantics></math>. During adaptation, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> remains frozen, and only <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are trained. The modified forward pass for a hidden state <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=Wx+\Delta Wx=Wx+BAx." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=Wx+\Delta Wx=Wx+BAx.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For initialization, the matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is typically initialized with the Kaiming Uniform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. 2015</a>)</cite>, while <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized to zero, ensuring that <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=BA</annotation></semantics></math> is zero at the beginning of training.
Functionally, the LoRA module can be conceptualized as a two-stage process: feature compression and decompression.
The matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a projector or compressor, mapping the high-dimensional input features into a low-rank, compact space.
Subsequently, the matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a restorer or decompressor, projecting these low-rank features back to the original output dimension space.
For stable training, the LoRA update is typically scaled by a constant <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha}{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a learning rate-like scalar.
Thus, the final forward pass is <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=W_{0}x+\frac{\alpha}{r}BAx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=W_{0}x+\frac{\alpha}{r}BAx</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Representational Bottleneck of Single-<math id="Sx3.SSx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> LoRA</h3>

<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Information Ceiling)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any input <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">in</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with covariance
matrix <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{x}\succeq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{x}\succeq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the down‑projection
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{u}}=A\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{u}}=A\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}})</annotation></semantics></math> in a
rank‑<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> LoRA layer satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="Sx3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\mathbf{x};B\mathbf{u})\leq\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\bigl(I_{r}+A\Sigma_{x}A^{\top}\bigr),\newline
\text{rank}(\mathbf{u})\leq r," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\mathbf{x};B\mathbf{u})\leq\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\bigl(I_{r}+A\Sigma_{x}A^{\top}\bigr),\newline
\text{rank}(\mathbf{u})\leq r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for <em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">any</em> deterministic post‑mapping <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (even multiple
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>‑heads). Thus, a <em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">single‑<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></em> LoRA can transmit at most
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal information channels.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof (sketch).</h6>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\!=\!B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\!=\!B</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic,
<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\!\to\!{\mathbf{u}}\!\to\!g({\mathbf{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\!\to\!{\mathbf{u}}\!\to\!g({\mathbf{u}})</annotation></semantics></math> forms a Markov chain;
The data‑processing inequality yields the information bound.
The rank bound follows from <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{rank}(A)=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{rank}(A)=r</annotation></semantics></math>. The full proof is provided in Appendix A.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark.</span> Replacing <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> by an ensemble
<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and aggregating
<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{u}}=\sum_{i}A_{i}\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{u}}=\sum_{i}A_{i}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the rank
constraint, but increases
<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(I_{r}+\!\sum_{i}\!A_{i}\Sigma_{x}A_{i}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.026em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.026em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(I_{r}+\!\sum_{i}\!A_{i}\Sigma_{x}A_{i}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>,
thereby lifting the ceiling in (<a href="#Sx3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Theorem 1 (Information Ceiling). ‣ Representational Bottleneck of Single-𝐴 LoRA ‣ The Proposed Method ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
This insight motivates our “multi‑<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, single‑<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” design, which diversifies the feature extractor
without inflating projection heads.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">The Multi-<math id="Sx3.SSx3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Expert (MAE) Block</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed above, the single matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in standard LoRA and its asymmetric variants (<span id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, HydraLoRA) may become a representational bottleneck, limiting the model’s capacity to extract diverse and nuanced features. Our strategy is to reallocate the extra capacity to the feature extraction stage.
The Multi-<math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Expert (MAE) block replaces the single matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with an ensemble of <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> expert matrices, <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{1},A_{2},\dots,A_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{1},A_{2},\dots,A_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These experts operate in parallel, enabling the model to capture more diverse and fine-grained features from the input. We hypothesize that each expert matrix learns to specialize in distinct semantic subspaces. This functional specialization is empirically supported by t-SNE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maaten and Hinton 2008</a>)</cite> visualizations in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which illustrate that different <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices map the same inputs to separable feature clusters.
The outputs of these experts are aggregated and then projected back to the original dimension by a single restoration matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We aggregate expert features via summation, a simple yet effective approach that maintains parameter efficiency and training stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. </a>)</cite>, with detailed rationale provided in Appendix B.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{\text{MAE}}=B\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{i}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>MAE</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{\text{MAE}}=B\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This “multi-<math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, single-<math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” design explicitly reallocates model capacity towards the crucial feature extraction stage, hypothesizing that a richer set of initial feature representations is more beneficial for complex tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Asymmetric Cross-layer Sharing</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate the potential parameter increase from using multiple <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices and to reduce their redundancy across transformer layers, we introduce an asymmetric cross-layer sharing mechanism for efficient adaptation. Unlike other sharing methods (VB-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, Han, and Ji 2024</a>)</cite>, BSLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. 2025</a>)</cite>) that typically share the symmetric LoRA module (both <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>), our approach introduces an asymmetric sharing strategy: we only share the ensemble of <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices across adjacent layers, while allowing each layer to retain its own independent, trainable <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To substantiate our design, we conducted an empirical analysis of inter-layer activation similarity using Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kornblith et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, a robust method for comparing neural network representations.
As presented in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Introduction ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we applied CKA to measure the similarity between activation values from adjacent layers of a fine-tuned model, specifically comparing the <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix outputs (<math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ax</annotation></semantics></math>) against the final LoRA increment activations (<math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BAx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BAx</annotation></semantics></math>). The analysis reveals a distinct pattern: The <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix outputs maintain a significantly higher similarity across layers than the full increments. This observation is quantified by the aggregated plots, which confirm a consistently higher average similarity score for the <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix outputs across all attention projections (<math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q,k,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q,k,v</annotation></semantics></math>). This evidence indicates that 1) <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices learn to produce generalizable cross-layer representations, making them ideal candidates for sharing; and 2) <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices, conversely, perform more layer-specific transformations, which are critical for preserving the unique functionality of each layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this empirical evidence,
we group consecutive layers into blocks of size <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Within each block <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, all layers share the same ensemble of <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices, <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{1}^{(k)},\dots,A_{N}^{(k)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{1}^{(k)},\dots,A_{N}^{(k)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, each layer <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> retains its own independent, trainable <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix, denoted as <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach leverages the generalizability of <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices for substantial parameter savings, while the layer-specific <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices preserve the model’s capacity for nuanced, per-layer adaptation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">The Whole MASA Framework</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compromise performance and efficiency, we combine the Multi-<math id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Expert block with our asymmetric sharing strategy to form the complete framework, <span id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ulti-<math id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>hared <span id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptation (MASA). The overall architecture and data flow are illustrated in Fig. <a href="#Sx2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Cross-Layer Parameter Sharing Strategies ‣ Related Work ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a given layer <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> in a MASA-adapted model, the modified forward pass is defined by its independent restoration matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the shared group of <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}^{(k)}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}^{(k)}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The complete mathematical formulation is as follows:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(l)}=W_{0}^{(l)}x+\frac{\alpha}{r}B^{(l)}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{i}^{(k)}\right)x,\quad\text{where }k=\lfloor l/S\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(l)}=W_{0}^{(l)}x+\frac{\alpha}{r}B^{(l)}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{i}^{(k)}\right)x,\quad\text{where }k=\lfloor l/S\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the index of the shared <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix group, determined by the layer index <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> (0-indexed) and the size of sharing group <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter Complexity Analysis:</span> For a model with <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers, the number of trainable parameters in MASA is approximately <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{L}{S}\times N\times d_{in}\times r+L\times d_{out}\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{L}{S}\times N\times d_{in}\times r+L\times d_{out}\times r</annotation></semantics></math>. The sharing mechanism significantly reduces the cost associated with the <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices, which would otherwise be the largest contributor due to multiple experts (<math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>). This makes MASA’s parameter count comparable to or even lower than standard LoRA (<math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\times(d_{in}+d_{out})\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\times(d_{in}+d_{out})\times r</annotation></semantics></math>), especially when <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is large. For example, on LLaMA3-8B, MASA in the <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3A1B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3A1B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=2</annotation></semantics></math> configuration reduces <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> 36.3% parameters than HydraLoRA in the corresponding <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1A3B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1A3B</annotation></semantics></math> configuration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
