<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of zero-shot performance (%) of our proposed MASA method against other PEFT techniques on LLaMA3-8B. Performance is reported as accuracy on the MMLU benchmark and three single-domain tasks. The <math id="Sx4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> specify the number of <math id="Sx4.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices in the respective LoRA architectures. The best accuracy in each column is highlighted in <span id="Sx4.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.
The results based on LLaMA3.2-3B are in Appendix D.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experimental Setups</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Benchmarks.</span>
All experiments are conducted on the pretrained LLaMA3-8B, LLaMA3.1-8B and LLaMA3.2-3B models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
To evaluate multi-domain generalization, we fine-tune the model on the databricks-dolly-15k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conover et al. 2023</a>)</cite> dataset and assess its performance on the Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. 2020</a>)</cite>.
For single-domain specialization, we focus on three distinct professional fields. In Law, the model is fine-tuned on Lawyer-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alignment-Lab-AI 2024</a>)</cite> and US-Terms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023</a>)</cite> and evaluated on the law-related tasks within MMLU. For Math, we use the training set of GSM8k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. 2021</a>)</cite> for fine-tuning and its test set for evaluation. Finally, in the Finance domain, we fine-tune on the training set of Fingpt-fineval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang, Yang, and Wang 2023</a>)</cite> and test on its corresponding evaluation set. For multi-task reasoning, we fine-tune the model on a subset of the OpenOrca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lian et al. 2023</a>)</cite> dataset and evaluate its performance on the Big-Bench Hard (BBH) benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzgun et al. 2022</a>)</cite>.
Further details regarding these datasets are provided in Appendix C.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation details.</span>
Unless otherwise noted, we fix the LoRA rank to <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=8</annotation></semantics></math>, scaling factor to <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=16</annotation></semantics></math>, number of <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>‑experts to <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and the size of sharing group to <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
All models are fine‑tuned with AdamW (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{lr}=5^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>lr</mtext><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{lr}=5^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{batch size}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>batch size</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{batch size}=8</annotation></semantics></math>).
A full hyper‑parameter table is provided in Appendix C.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
To ensure a comprehensive evaluation, we compare the proposed MASA against representative methods spanning different PEFT paradigms:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Sx4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt and Representation Tuning: Methods that leave the backbone weights untouched and instead learn continuous prompts or prefix vectors: Prompt Tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lester, Al-Rfou, and Constant 2021</a>)</cite> and P<span id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2021</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Single–Adapter LoRA Family: All methods insert one lightweight adapter per modified module, differing only in how the low‑rank update is parameterised: LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, DoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, VB‑LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, Han, and Ji 2024</a>)</cite>, and BS‑LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. 2025</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi–Adapter and Modular LoRA: Approaches that instantiate multiple LoRA‑style adapters and decide at run‑time how to combine them: LoRAMoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, HydraLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, MTL‑LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, and CoLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, Yao, and Chen 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx4.T2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="Sx4.T2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
