<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Impact of varying the counts of <math id="Sx4.T4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices (<em id="Sx4.T4.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em>, <math id="Sx4.T4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="Sx4.T4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> projections (<em id="Sx4.T4.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em>, <math id="Sx4.T4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>) on MMLU accuracy and parameter overhead. Experiments show that accuracy peaks at <math id="Sx4.T4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}5</annotation></semantics></math> and then declines; increasing <math id="Sx4.T4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> beyond one reduces accuracy while almost doubling the extra parameters, confirming the efficiency of the “5<math id="Sx4.T4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>–1<math id="Sx4.T4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” design.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of optimal <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> configuration.</span>
We systematically explored the impact of different combinations of <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices on the adaptation performance. As summarized in Tab. <a href="#Sx4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ Experiments ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we present the model’s accuracy on the MMLU benchmark as a function of various (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>) configurations.
<em id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Scaling <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
Increasing <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> improves MMLU performance to 59.62% with the modest parameter growth, while further scaling to <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}6</annotation></semantics></math> declines the performance, indicating the performance saturation. <em id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Scaling <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
In contrast, increasing <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> nearly doubles the parameter overhead while slightly reducing accuracy. This suggests that multiple <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> projections introduce the redundancy to decrease performance.
These results validate the “multi‑<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, single‑<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” principle: a small ensemble of <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices can enrich the representation space, whereas duplicating <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> mostly inflates the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed effect of ASC.</span>
We further validate the rationale and optimality of our ASC. As the heatmaps in the methodology section revealed, <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices exhibit higher inter-layer similarity than <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, which inspired our design to share only the <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. To directly prove the superiority of this decision in terms of experimental performance, we conducted a comparative experiment across three different sharing strategies, which are summarized in Tab. <a href="#Sx4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ Experiments ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The results confirm that our design is well-motivated. The “Share <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>” strategy achieves a favorable balance between performance and efficiency. In contrast, “Share <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>” leads to a significant performance drop, suggesting the importance of layer-specific <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices for model expressiveness. Despite sharing both <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> improving parameter efficiency, it degrades performance from 59.62% to 59.51%, indicating that excessive sharing of <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> may hinder layer-specific transformations. Overall, Asymmetric Cross-layer Sharing on the <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices will be the best selection for adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To optimize our asymmetric sharing strategy, we evaluate different sharing group sizes (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=1,2,4,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=1,2,4,8</annotation></semantics></math>). As shown in Tab. <a href="#Sx4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ Experiments ‣ MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=2</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the best trade-off, yielding the highest MMLU accuracy (59.62%) with only a 0.522% learnable parameter. Larger <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> values (4, 8) enhance efficiency but significantly reduce performance. These results validate that sharing <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices across adjacent layers (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=2</annotation></semantics></math>) is the optimal selection for MASA.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx4.T5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="Sx4.T5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sharing Strategy</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="Sx4.T5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU Avg.</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="Sx4.T5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Params. (%)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MASA</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T5.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">59.62</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T5.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.5220</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Share <math id="Sx4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math id="Sx4.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">59.19</td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.7605</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Share Both <math id="Sx4.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">59.51</td>
<td id="Sx4.T5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.4541</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Effect of our Asymmetric Sharing strategy comparing against other potential sharing schemes.</figcaption>
</figure>
